<p>President Donald Trump is hosting the first meeting of his new “Board of Peace” in Washington on Feb. 19, bringing together representatives from more than two dozen countries. The group’s first focus is Gaza’s future, including reconstruction and humanitarian assistance. The White House said member states have pledged more than $5 billion for reconstruction and humanitarian efforts and committed “thousands of personnel” to an international stabilization force for Gaza. U.S. partners are responding unevenly. Several Western democracies are staying out or keeping their involvement limited, reflecting concerns about how the new body fits alongside existing international institutions. Some countries and officials are attending as observers rather than formally joining. Diplomacy around Gaza is also moving on a parallel track at the UN. The Security Council held a high-level meeting on the Gaza ceasefire and discussed Israel’s plans regarding the occupied West Bank ahead of the Washington gathering, highlighting that multiple negotiations and pressure points remain in play.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/BD4oWNbMbqUTwIxmUCf3xuaEXgrtZ1X0US9ekp4vYi0Gnn6tod-6iyLuojO82g56BpZIOJ2CXuSpetceVexAnwlUGFkq9GOyKJWHmSqEr8VUaVtIp3rhzFCAliNiVHvUHipt5GIQtKL0_f_EyFirimYwsyE4IrteM8fNDvt-U5CwSmjVMdPYSThN-MKbgeCR-Yo9WBwUNSF2xJ6LiN2VykNNTaxiGXG9Kcdqc9LQIzsfSS0uVmlDl4gddwv42UsJ7Jspe-NcG0zW0jvlug' alt='U.S. President Donald Trump speaks during the Board of Peace charter announcement at Davos on Jan. 22, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Participant profile: Reports said the inaugural meeting drew some leaders closely aligned with Trump — including several authoritarian-leaning partners — while many traditional European democratic allies stayed away.</li><li>Scale vs. decisions: Preview coverage expected representatives from dozens of nations, even as key questions about Gaza’s postwar governance and administration remain unresolved.</li><li>Second phase: The U.S. says the plan is moving into a “second phase” focused on disarming Hamas.</li><li>EU positioning: POLITICO said the European Commission planned to send Mediterranean Commissioner Dubravka Šuica while stopping short of signing on to the initiative.</li><li>UN parallel track: Ahead of the Washington meeting, Security Council members used a high-level ceasefire session to warn that Israel’s West Bank plans could fuel wider instability as Gaza negotiations continue.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: The White House said member states pledged billions of dollars for Gaza reconstruction and humanitarian efforts and committed personnel to an international stabilization force. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/trump-host-board-peace-meeting-thursday-white-house-says'>Al-Monitor (Reuters)</a>)</li><li>Vatican: Cardinal Pietro Parolin said the Vatican will not take part, adding that efforts to manage crises should be handled by the United Nations. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/vatican-says-it-will-not-participate-in-trumps-board-of-peace'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>European Union: Le Monde reported EU capitals are split, with only a limited number taking formal seats while other countries — and the European Commission — opted to send observers. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/18/europe-is-divided-over-trump-s-board-of-peace_6750622_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Analysts (Al Jazeera): Al Jazeera reported analysts expect President Trump to use the first meeting as a “proof of concept,” and said progress would require pressure on Israel. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/19/proof-of-concept-what-trump-can-achieve-in-first-board-of-peace-meeting'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Foreign-Policy/2026/0218/trump-board-of-peace-gaza-reconstruction-israel'>Trump’s Board of Peace meets, facing wariness and an immediate test: Gaza